Native navigation tells assistive technology that a new page has loaded. In a SPA the document is the same, so the application must supply those cues.

{{diagram:route-focus}}

## What must happen

| Need | Technique |
|---|---|
| Unique, descriptive page title | Update `document.title` per route (WCAG 2.4.2) |
| Keyboard focus continues logically | Move focus to the new content's heading or container |
| Screen reader hears the change | Focus move on a heading often suffices; a live region can supplement |
| User can bypass repeated blocks | Skip link to `main` (WCAG 2.4.1) |
| Back and forward restore position | Preserve or restore focus and scroll thoughtfully |

## React example

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef } from "react";
import { useLocation } from "react-router-dom";

export function RouteFocus({ title }: { title: string }) {
  const headingRef = useRef<HTMLHeadingElement>(null);
  const { pathname } = useLocation();
  const first = useRef(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = `${title} | Shop`;
    if (first.current) { first.current = false; return; } // do not steal focus on first load
    headingRef.current?.focus();
  }, [pathname, title]);

  return <h1 ref={headingRef} tabIndex={-1}>{title}</h1>;
}
```

`tabIndex={-1}` makes the heading programmatically focusable without adding it to the tab order. Avoid a visible oversized outline on the heading if it confuses sighted users, but do not remove focus indication from interactive elements.

## Next.js notes

The App Router includes a route announcer that reads the new page's title or first `h1`. Treat it as a baseline: behaviour has varied across versions, and it does not decide where keyboard focus lands, so test your own flow.

## Edge cases

- Modal or drawer routes: focus into the dialog, return on close.
- Same-page updates such as filters: use a live region for result counts rather than moving focus.
- Error pages and redirects need the same treatment.

## Common mistakes

- Leaving focus on the clicked link that has since unmounted, so focus drops to the body.
- Moving focus on initial load and skipping the page's natural start.
- Identical titles for every route.
- Relying on a single browser and screen reader pair when verifying.

WCAG 2.1 **4.1.3 Status Messages (AA)** requires that status messages can be programmatically determined and presented by assistive technology **without receiving focus**.

{{diagram:live-region}}

## Options

| Mechanism | Behaviour | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| `role="status"` (implicit `aria-live="polite"`) | Announced when the user is idle | Added to basket, filters applied, saved |
| `role="alert"` (assertive) | Interrupts | Blocking errors, session expiring |
| `aria-live="polite"` on a container | Same as status, more flexible | Custom regions |
| `aria-atomic` | Read the whole region or only the change | Counters |
| Moving focus | Not a live region; use for dialogs and new pages | Major context changes |

## React implementation

```tsx
import { useState } from "react";

export function BasketButton({ name }: { name: string }) {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState("");

  function add() {
    // ... add to basket
    setMessage(`${name} added to basket`);
  }

  return (
    <>
      <button type="button" onClick={add}>Add to basket</button>
      <div role="status" className="visually-hidden">{message}</div>
    </>
  );
}
```

Mount the status container at app or layout level so it persists. An element that is inserted **together with** its text is frequently not announced, because the region did not exist beforehand.

## Practical rules

1. Announce outcomes, not decorative changes.
2. Keep text brief and meaningful: "12 results" beats a paragraph.
3. Clear or change text so repeated identical messages can re-announce; some implementations need a small variation or a toggled node.
4. Do not put `aria-live` on large, frequently updating regions such as tickers.
5. Toasts that disappear must give users enough time (WCAG 2.2.1) or be persistent, and must not hold the only copy of an error.

## Common mistakes

- Using `role="alert"` for everything, creating a noisy experience.
- Injecting a new live region with its message in one render.
- Visually hiding the message with `display: none`, which removes it from the accessibility tree; use a visually-hidden class.
- Assuming one screen reader's output generalises to all.

Relevant WCAG criteria include 1.3.1 Info and Relationships, 1.3.5 Identify Input Purpose, 3.3.1 Error Identification, 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions, 3.3.3 Error Suggestion, 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, and in 2.2 also 3.3.7 Redundant Entry.

## Example

```tsx
import { useRef, useState } from "react";

type Errors = Partial<Record<"name" | "postcode", string>>;

export function AddressForm() {
  const [errors, setErrors] = useState<Errors>({});
  const summaryRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  function onSubmit(e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const data = new FormData(e.currentTarget);
    const next: Errors = {};
    if (!String(data.get("name")).trim()) next.name = "Enter your full name";
    if (!/^[A-Za-z0-9 ]{5,8}$/.test(String(data.get("postcode"))))
      next.postcode = "Enter a postcode, for example SW1A 1AA";
    setErrors(next);
    if (Object.keys(next).length) setTimeout(() => summaryRef.current?.focus(), 0);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={onSubmit} noValidate>
      {Object.keys(errors).length > 0 && (
        <div ref={summaryRef} tabIndex={-1} role="alert" className="error-summary">
          <h2>There is a problem</h2>
          <ul>
            {Object.entries(errors).map(([id, msg]) => (
              <li key={id}><a href={`#${id}`}>{msg}</a></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      <label htmlFor="name">Full name <span>(required)</span></label>
      <input id="name" name="name" autoComplete="name" required
        aria-invalid={!!errors.name}
        aria-describedby={errors.name ? "name-err" : undefined} />
      {errors.name && <p id="name-err" className="error">Error: {errors.name}</p>}

      <label htmlFor="postcode">Postcode <span>(required)</span></label>
      <input id="postcode" name="postcode" autoComplete="postal-code" required
        aria-invalid={!!errors.postcode}
        aria-describedby={errors.postcode ? "postcode-err" : "postcode-hint"} />
      <p id="postcode-hint">For example SW1A 1AA</p>
      {errors.postcode && <p id="postcode-err" className="error">Error: {errors.postcode}</p>}

      <button type="submit">Continue to payment</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

The summary links must target focusable fields (anchors to an `id` move focus in most browsers; in a SPA you may need to call `.focus()` in a click handler).

## Checklist

| Point | Why |
|---|---|
| Visible `<label>`, not placeholder | Placeholders vanish and often fail contrast |
| `autocomplete` tokens | Meets 1.3.5; helps users with motor and cognitive disabilities |
| Text cue for required and for errors | Colour alone fails 1.4.1 |
| Error text includes the fix | 3.3.3 |
| `noValidate` with custom validation | Native bubbles are inconsistent across assistive technology, so choose one approach deliberately |
| Group related inputs with `fieldset` and `legend` | Radio sets and address blocks |
| Do not clear entered data on error | 3.3.7 spirit |

## Common mistakes

- Setting `aria-invalid` before the user has interacted.
- Error messages not associated with the field.
- Moving focus to the first field without any explanation of why.
- Disabling the submit button, hiding why it is unavailable.

{{diagram:a11y-test-layers}}

## Tool roles

| Tool | Where it runs | Good for | Limits |
|---|---|---|---|
| eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y | Editor and CI | Static JSX mistakes | No runtime context |
| jest-axe or similar | Unit and component tests | Rendered component markup | Misses page-level and interaction issues |
| Playwright with `@axe-core/playwright` | End-to-end in CI | Real pages, states after interaction | Only scans the state you reach |
| Lighthouse | DevTools, CI | Quick overview, trend scores | Subset of axe rules; a score of 100 is not conformance |
| Browser extensions | Manual | Guided checks, colour contrast | Same engine limits |
| Storybook addon | Design system | Per-component feedback | Components only |

```ts
// Playwright with axe
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";
import AxeBuilder from "@axe-core/playwright";

test("checkout page has no detectable WCAG A/AA violations", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/checkout");
  const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
    .withTags(["wcag2a", "wcag2aa", "wcag21aa", "wcag22aa"])
    .analyze();
  expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
});
```

Tag names depend on the axe version, so confirm which WCAG 2.2 rules your installed version supports.

## Why automation is not enough

Tools can detect missing alt attributes, low contrast on rendered text, missing labels, duplicate ids and invalid ARIA. They cannot determine:

- whether alt text is accurate or appropriate;
- whether focus order and reading order make sense;
- whether a custom widget actually works with a keyboard and a screen reader;
- whether error messages are understandable;
- whether captions are correct;
- states they never reach (open menus, error states, modals).

A passing scan therefore means "no automatically detectable failures in the tested states", **not** "WCAG conformant". Never claim conformance from tool output alone; use it to prevent regressions and combine it with manual evaluation.

## Practical setup

1. Fail the build on new serious or critical violations, with a documented, time-boxed baseline for known ones.
2. Scan key journeys in several states, at mobile and desktop widths, with zoom.
3. Record manual results in the same tracker.

## Common mistakes

- Treating a Lighthouse score as a compliance badge.
- Suppressing rules to turn the build green.
- Only testing the initial page state.

## Relevant criteria

| Criterion | Level | Requirement |
|---|---|---|
| 1.4.4 Resize Text | AA | Text resizable to 200 percent without loss of content |
| 1.4.10 Reflow | AA | No two-dimensional scrolling at 320 CSS pixel width (equivalent to 400 percent zoom at 1280), except content such as data tables, maps and diagrams that need two dimensions |
| 1.4.12 Text Spacing | AA | No loss of content when users set line height, paragraph, letter and word spacing within stated values |
| 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus | AA | Dismissible, hoverable, persistent |
| 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide | A | Controls for moving content over five seconds |
| 2.3.1 Three Flashes | A | Nothing flashes more than three times per second beyond thresholds |
| 2.3.3 Animation from Interactions | AAA | Motion triggered by interaction can be disabled; supported in practice by reduced-motion CSS |

## Implementation

```css
/* Fluid, wrapping layout */
.layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }

/* Avoid fixed heights that clip enlarged text */
.card { min-height: 0; padding: 1rem; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

/* Respect motion preference */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after { animation-duration: 0.01ms !important; transition-duration: 0.01ms !important; scroll-behavior: auto !important; }
}
```

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";
export function usePrefersReducedMotion() {
  const [reduced, setReduced] = useState(false);
  useEffect(() => {
    const mq = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");
    setReduced(mq.matches);
    const on = (e: MediaQueryListEvent) => setReduced(e.matches);
    mq.addEventListener("change", on);
    return () => mq.removeEventListener("change", on);
  }, []);
  return reduced;
}
```

## Practices

- Use `rem` and `em` for type and spacing so browser font settings apply; avoid `user-scalable=no` or `maximum-scale=1` in the viewport meta tag.
- Sticky headers and cookie banners can consume the whole viewport at high zoom; allow them to collapse or become non-sticky (2.4.11).
- Truncating text with ellipsis can hide content; offer the full text.
- Carousels and auto-playing animations need pause controls and should not auto-advance when reduced motion is requested.
- Test by zooming the browser to 400 percent and with a text-spacing bookmarklet or extension.

## Common mistakes

- Fixed pixel heights and `overflow: hidden` on text containers.
- Testing responsiveness only by dragging the window.
- Treating reduced motion as turning off all feedback instead of replacing large movement with subtle alternatives.

ARIA has **roles** (what it is), **states** (changing conditions such as `aria-expanded`, `aria-selected`) and **properties** (relationships such as `aria-labelledby`, `aria-controls`). It adds meaning but **no behaviour**: `role="button"` on a div does not make it focusable or respond to Space.

## Rules of ARIA, summarised

1. Use a native element or attribute when one exists.
2. Do not change native semantics unless you must.
3. All interactive ARIA controls must be keyboard operable.
4. Do not hide focusable elements with `aria-hidden` or `role="presentation"`.
5. Every interactive element needs an accessible name.

## Tabs example

```tsx
import { useRef, useState, KeyboardEvent } from "react";

const tabs = [
  { id: "overview", label: "Overview", body: "Overview content" },
  { id: "specs", label: "Specifications", body: "Specs content" },
  { id: "reviews", label: "Reviews", body: "Reviews content" },
];

export function Tabs() {
  const [active, setActive] = useState(0);
  const refs = useRef<(HTMLButtonElement | null)[]>([]);

  function onKeyDown(e: KeyboardEvent) {
    const last = tabs.length - 1;
    let next = active;
    if (e.key === "ArrowRight") next = active === last ? 0 : active + 1;
    else if (e.key === "ArrowLeft") next = active === 0 ? last : active - 1;
    else if (e.key === "Home") next = 0;
    else if (e.key === "End") next = last;
    else return;
    e.preventDefault();
    setActive(next);
    refs.current[next]?.focus();
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <div role="tablist" aria-label="Product information" onKeyDown={onKeyDown}>
        {tabs.map((t, i) => (
          <button key={t.id} id={`tab-${t.id}`} role="tab" type="button"
            ref={el => (refs.current[i] = el)}
            aria-selected={i === active} aria-controls={`panel-${t.id}`}
            tabIndex={i === active ? 0 : -1}
            onClick={() => setActive(i)}>{t.label}</button>
        ))}
      </div>
      {tabs.map((t, i) => (
        <div key={t.id} id={`panel-${t.id}`} role="tabpanel"
          aria-labelledby={`tab-${t.id}`} hidden={i !== active} tabIndex={0}>
          {t.body}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Key ideas

| Idea | Detail |
|---|---|
| Roving tabindex | Only the active tab has `tabIndex=0`; Tab leaves the widget, arrows move within |
| Automatic vs manual activation | Selecting on arrow focus is simple; use manual activation if panels are slow to load |
| Syncing state | `aria-selected` derives from React state, never separate |
| Hidden panels | Use `hidden` so inactive panels leave the accessibility tree |

## Common mistakes

- Adding `role="tab"` without arrow-key handling.
- Using `aria-label` to override visible text.
- Setting `aria-hidden="true"` on focusable content.
- Copying an APG pattern partially, which creates promises the widget does not keep.
- Using ARIA when a simple list of links would serve better.
